I'm adding a new kernel which has a different computation for X (input) and Z (inducing points).
Assume K_uu is the kernel function for the inducing inputs (e.g. z1 and z2) and K_ff in the kernel function for the inputs (e.g. x1 and x2). In my implementation, I'm extending the Kernel class and implementing my own forward function. Inputs to this function are x1 and x2 - now I need to distinguish inside this function whether the inputs belong to X or Z so that I compute and return the correct K there, essentially one of K_uu, K_ff, K_uf or K_uf
In RBF for example, all of these quantities are the same - so there is no distinction between normal data points and inducing data points, everything is much simpler there - in my case, I'm not sure how to proceed...
Is there an easy way of identifying if x1 and x2 belong to normal data points or inducing data points in the forward function of a kernel?
Ideally I need something like this pseudo code:
    def forward(self, x1, x2, diag=False, **params):
        if x1 & x2 both belong to input points:
            return kff(x1, x2)
        elif x1 & x2 both belong to inducing points:
            return kuu(x1, x2)
        elif x1 belongs to input points and x2 belongs to inducing points:
            return kfu(x1, x2)
        else:
            return kuf(x1, x2)

(I can implement a hacky version of this to store and index all X values and then do some sort of lookup here - but that does't seem very elegant and seems really hacky)
Any suggestions is really appreciated!

